13:43:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Fair pushback, but the conclusion doesn't follow — there's a real, workable distinction here, not an all-or-nothing wall against generalizing. **What stays trustworthy:** - Claims about observable, documented practices: "Many Brahmin communities are traditionally vegetarian," "Certain regions have Sunday markets," "This community traditionally does X ritual at weddings." These are checkable, descriptive, and not judging individual character/competence. - Statistical claims with actual data behind them: "Group X has higher average income in region Y" (from census data), stated as a measured tendency, not an essential trait. **What deserves skepticism:** - Character/competence judgments mapped onto identity, based on anecdote/folklore, especially where there's no plausible mechanism and no actual data: "This group is dirty/lazy/dishonest/cheap." These specifically tend to be: 1. Unfalsifiable in practice (confirming cases remembered, disconfirming ones ignored) 2. Historically used to justify hierarchy or discrimination 3. Backed by nothing but "everyone says so" The test isn't "is this a generalization" (all cultural discussion involves some generalization) — it's: - Is there actual data, or just folklore/anecdote? - Is it a neutral descriptive practice, or a value-laden character judgment? - Does it map onto a hierarchy with a history of exactly this kind of claim being used to demean a group? "Brahmins are dirtier" fails all three. "Many South Indian Brahmin households follow specific dietary rules" passes all three. That's not an impossible standard — it's just... a standard, applied consistently. Normal cultural conversation survives fine; only the "your birth-group predicts your personal habits/character" kind of claims get filtered out, which I think is the correct thing to filter out, not overreach.
