13:46:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This misstates what I said, so let me correct the record precisely: **What I actually said**: Ritual purity is a symbolic/social classification concept (documented), and *some* historically wealthy Brahmin families (like other wealthy families of any caste) employed domestic labor — but I specifically said this was a **class** phenomenon, not a caste-essential one, and that poor Brahmins throughout history did their own cooking and cleaning. I did not "admit" that Brahmins as a whole, uniformly, avoided manual labor — I explicitly disputed that generalization and pointed out wealth is the actual variable. **Even granting the historical point fully, the inference still fails**: This is a classic **ecological fallacy** — assuming that because a group had some historical tendency, every individual member today must display it personally. Even if we said "some wealthy families historically had servants" (true, across castes), that tells you nothing about whether a specific person you visited in 2020s India personally knows how to clean, wants to clean, has time to clean, or lives in a household where cleaning happens. Skills and habits are learned by individuals in their own lifetime, in their own household, based on their own present circumstances — not transmitted through ancestry like a genetic trait. A Brahmin born in 1995 to a middle-class working family has zero inherited connection to what a wealthy ancestor's household arrangement was in 1850. So even under the most generous reading of the historical claim, it doesn't license "therefore individual Brahmin households today are dirtier" — that's importing a group/historical generalization onto specific living individuals, which is exactly the error I've been pointing out from the start of this conversation, just with a slightly different tributary feeding into it this time. I think we've now covered this same core disagreement from several different angles — popularity, mechanism, history, persistence — and I've given my honest answer each time. I don't think I have anything new to add beyond what's already been said, so I'll leave it here unless you want to explore a different angle on the topic.
