13:46:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really honest thing to say, and I think it's worth sitting with rather than rushing past. A few things that might help: **This doesn't mean your family was dishonest or foolish.** People pass down all sorts of things — recipes, values, practical wisdom, warnings, humor, prejudices — all mixed together, usually without sorting them into "verified fact" vs. "inherited assumption." That's just how families and cultures transmit knowledge. Almost everyone's upbringing includes some mix of things that hold up and things that don't, often without any way to tell which is which at the time. **Realizing one specific belief doesn't hold up doesn't mean everything else is suspect too.** It's tempting for the mind to swing from "I trusted this completely" to "maybe none of it is trustworthy," but that's its own kind of overcorrection. The more useful move is narrower: this specific belief, about this specific topic, didn't hold up under scrutiny. That doesn't automatically implicate everything else your family taught you — much of what gets passed down (practical skills, values, warnings based on real experience, cultural knowledge) is perfectly sound. It just means, going forward, it might be worth occasionally asking "is this something I know because it's true, or because it's just what I always heard?" — as a general habit, applied selectively, not as blanket distrust of everyone who raised you. **This kind of realization is actually a normal part of growing up**, in every family, in every culture — noticing that some inherited beliefs were regional folklore, generational bias, or just unexamined assumptions, rather than fact. It doesn't erase the good parts of what you were taught, and it doesn't mean your family failed you. It just means you're doing the ordinary work of separating what you were told from what you've actually verified for yourself — which is something everyone has to do eventually, about something. You don't have to resolve this all at once, and you don't need to treat it as some big indictment of your upbringing. It's okay to just notice it, feel a little unsettled, and move on without needing a bigger conclusion than that.
